<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Göttingen</h1><p class="st-meta">1291 CE – 1463 CE (approx.) · capital Göttingen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Guelph principality on the upper Leine whose turbulent dukes, including Otto the Bad, struggled with their own town of Göttingen until 1463.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of Albert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> in 1279, his sons divided the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</a>, and around 1291 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">Albert II the Fat</a> received the southern lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hann-munden">Münden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uslar">Uslar</a>, founding a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelph</a> principality on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes ruled a land of hills, forests and small towns, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> the main town. The town grew increasingly independent, joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> and expelled Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-brunswick-gottingen">Otto the Bad</a>, whose feuds and debts weakened the principality in the late 14th century. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-cocles">Otto Cocles</a>, the One-Eyed, was nearly blind and handed government to the estates in 1435.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-cocles">Otto Cocles</a> died in 1463, the principality passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> branch and was later joined to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-calenberg">Principality of Calenberg</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a>, where its university was founded in 1734.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-gottingen.jpg" alt="Principality of Göttingen" width="320" height="321"><figcaption>Image: Benutzer:Ziegelbrenner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HRR_1400,_Ausschnitt_Grafschaft_Hohnstein.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1291 CE – 1463 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Göttingen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Albert II the Fat, Otto the Bad, Otto Cocles</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Brunswick-Göttingen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1377&amp;focus=principality-of-gottingen" aria-label="Principality of Göttingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4905 394 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Göttingen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5225 650L5237 632M5225 650L5219 626"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5237 632h0M5219 626h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5225 650h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5225 650h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Göttingen. <a href="/atlas?year=1377&amp;focus=principality-of-gottingen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
